const ROOT = '/home/user/cornwall-fishing-search'

const PREAMBLE = `CONTEXT (read fully). You are one research stream in a large, deliberately exhaustive study:
"Find the single best shore-fishing spot in Cornwall (UK) - the entire Cornish shoreline including every estuary and the Isles of Scilly - for one session on SUNDAY 22 NOVEMBER 2026 (any window within the 24 h; local time = GMT = UTC), any species, any method."
It is purely hypothetical: nobody is going. Locations are judged SOLELY on fishing quality. Ignore human factors completely (access, travel, danger, survivability, comfort, whether a person could physically stand there). Assume a superhuman angler who can reach and fish any point where land meets water - cliffs, islands, offshore rocks, reefs, harbour walls - in any conditions. Weather and sea matter only through their effect on fish and on presenting a bait or lure.
NOW: Saturday 21 November 2026, about 16:10 UTC. The session date is TOMORROW. The repository root is ${ROOT}.

HARD RULES
1. Never invent a tide time, forecast figure, catch report, regulation, coordinate or any other fact. Every figure must carry: source name + URL, the time (UTC) you accessed it, and the data's own date / model run / publication date. If something cannot be found after genuinely trying several sources, write "NOT FOUND" and list what you tried.
2. Grade every source: A = official / instrument / primary (UKHO, Met Office, buoy, legislation, model output); B = reputable secondary (established guide, tackle shop, club, magazine, pilot book); C = anecdotal (forum or social post, single blog); D = your own inference (say so and show the reasoning).
3. Separate observation from inference. Where data is thin write "THIN DATA" instead of guessing. Record disagreements between sources explicitly; do not average them away.
4. Depth over brevity: this is a stress test and there is no "enough". Record raw detail (tables, hour-by-hour figures, per-location rows), not just summaries - but do not pad with generic filler.
5. Files: write ONLY inside ${ROOT}/fishing/research/ and only the file(s) assigned to you below (create directories as needed). Save raw API responses / downloads you rely on under ${ROOT}/fishing/research/raw/<your-stream-key>/ with the fetch time in the filename. Do not modify other streams' files. Do not run git commands that change state (no add / commit / push / checkout / stash).
6. Write your file early and update it after every few findings, so that partial work survives if you are interrupted (an earlier run of every stream WAS interrupted by a usage limit at about 15:25 UTC).
7. RESUME: before doing anything else, check whether your assigned file(s) already exist and list ${ROOT}/fishing/research/raw/ for a directory belonging to your stream (same key, or a similar name). If they exist, read them and CONTINUE from that state - reuse the saved raw downloads, keep what is already written, do not start again from scratch.
8. TOKEN ECONOMY (strict): usage limits are tight. Never dump whole web pages or large JSON into your context. Save every download to disk and extract only what you need with python / grep / jq, printing short excerpts (aim for under 150 lines per tool output). Prefer one scripted batch of fetches over many single calls. Do not re-read files you have just written. Stop when further searching stops yielding new information.

TOOLS: Bash (curl works through a preconfigured proxy; never disable TLS verification; on TLS or proxy errors read /root/.ccr/README.md), python3 (pip install is allowed), Chromium + Playwright are pre-installed for JavaScript-rendered pages (node: require('playwright') works from any directory, chromium.launch() needs no options; do NOT run "playwright install"). WebSearch / WebFetch may be deferred tools: call ToolSearch with query "select:WebSearch,WebFetch" first to load whichever exist. If a site blocks automated fetches, use another source rather than hammering it. Put no personal details in any request; use a generic User-Agent.
Endpoints verified reachable at 15:06 UTC today: api.open-meteo.com (forecast API with many models; ensemble-api.open-meteo.com; archive-api.open-meteo.com), marine-api.open-meteo.com, easytide.admiralty.co.uk/Home/GetPredictionData?stationId=NNNN (UKHO predictions as JSON; 0002 returned Newlyn-area data covering 20 Nov onward), environment.data.gov.uk/flood-monitoring (EA river levels, rainfall, tide gauges), ows.emodnet-bathymetry.eu, ogcapi.bgs.ac.uk, html.duckduckgo.com. The main Overpass server failed; maps.mail.ru/osm/tools/overpass/api/interpreter worked.

STANDARD REFERENCE POINTS (use these names/coordinates so streams line up; add more as needed):
North coast: Bude 50.832,-4.556 | Tintagel Head 50.668,-4.763 | Port Isaac 50.595,-4.832 | Stepper Point (Camel mouth) 50.570,-4.945 | Trevose Head 50.549,-5.037 | Newquay Towan Head 50.425,-5.100 | Perranporth 50.350,-5.160 | St Agnes Head 50.320,-5.235 | Godrevy 50.240,-5.395 | St Ives Head 50.220,-5.478 | Pendeen Watch 50.165,-5.672 | Cape Cornwall 50.127,-5.709 | Land's End 50.066,-5.716
South coast: Gwennap Head 50.036,-5.680 | Newlyn/Penzance 50.100,-5.540 | Marazion 50.118,-5.477 | Porthleven/Loe Bar 50.070,-5.300 | Mullion 50.015,-5.260 | Lizard Point 49.958,-5.205 | Coverack/Manacles 50.045,-5.060 | Helford mouth 50.095,-5.095 | Falmouth Pendennis 50.145,-5.045 | Carrick Roads 50.21,-5.03 | Nare Head 50.195,-4.925 | Dodman Point 50.218,-4.802 | Mevagissey 50.270,-4.780 | Par 50.340,-4.705 | Fowey mouth 50.325,-4.645 | Polperro 50.328,-4.515 | Looe 50.350,-4.450 | Whitsand Bay 50.355,-4.290 | Rame Head 50.312,-4.223 | Saltash (Tamar) 50.405,-4.205
Scilly and offshore: St Mary's Hugh Town 49.915,-6.315 | St Agnes/Western Rocks 49.885,-6.370 | Bishop Rock 49.873,-6.445 | St Martin's 49.967,-6.270 | Wolf Rock 49.945,-5.808 | Longships 50.067,-5.747

RETURN VALUE: your final message is consumed by an orchestrator, not shown to a human. Return a dense summary (max ~600 words): the key findings with their key figures, explicit gaps / NOT FOUNDs, contradictions you noticed (within your stream or with what you would expect), and the file path(s) you wrote.`

const VERIFY = `You are an ADVERSARIAL VERIFIER. A researcher has just written the file(s) named below. Your job is to REFUTE them, not to admire them.
1. Read the file(s) in full.
2. Independently re-fetch or re-derive the most decision-critical figures from primary sources - at least 20 spot checks (all of them if fewer), preferring a different source from the one the researcher used. Open cited sources and confirm they really say what is claimed.
3. Check every date, time zone and unit: late-November UK clock time is GMT (no BST); knots vs mph vs m/s; chart datum vs ordnance datum; true vs magnetic bearings; wind/wave directions are "from" directions; model run times.
4. Hunt for invented, unsupported or stale statements, internal contradictions, and gaps relative to the stream brief.
5. Fix errors directly in the file, marking each correction inline as "[CORRECTED by verifier: was X]". Flag claims you could not verify in place as "[UNVERIFIED]". Add missing items you can source properly.
6. Append a "## Verification log" section: one row per check (claim, source used, time accessed, result = confirmed / corrected / unverifiable).
Default to "unverifiable" when uncertain - never rubber-stamp. Return: counts of confirmed / corrected / unverifiable, the most important corrections, and the remaining gaps.`

const MARKS_COMMON = `Build the LONGEST DEFENSIBLE list of documented shore-fishing marks for your sector. Go far beyond the famous venues: every named beach, cove, point, ledge, gully, zawn, reef, island, quay, pier, breakwater, slipway, pool, creek and river reach for which you can find ANY angling evidence (venue guides, magazine "where to fish" features, tackle-shop and club pages, match venue lists, forum threads, blogs, YouTube descriptions, books quoted online, record-fish lists that name the venue, mapping apps). Useful starting points (not exhaustive, and not all will exist): britishseafishing.co.uk venue pages, planetseafishing.com venue guides, seaangler.co.uk where-to-fish, worldseafishing.com forums and venue guides, cornwalls.co.uk fishing marks, intocornwall.com, cornishfederationofseaanglers / CFSA record lists (venue + species + weight + date), club sites (Mount's Bay AS, Bude, Newquay, Looe, Helston, Wadebridge etc.), The Fish Locker (YouTube, Cornwall), Henry Gilbey's blog, Art of Fishing (Wadebridge) blog, Cornish lure / LRF blogs, BASS blog, National Mullet Club, British Conger Club shore lists, fishing-marks websites and apps (Fishbrain, Fishing Points, UK sea fishing marks sites).
For EVERY mark record: name and aliases; coordinates (geocode with Nominatim/OSM or read off a cited source; give coord_confidence); type; the direction it faces; ground type (clean sand / shingle / mixed / rough / kelp reef / mud); depth (metres where sourced; "deep water close in" etc. otherwise); features (gullies, reefs, outflows, sandbanks, drop-offs, structure, lights); which wind and swell directions/heights it fishes WELL in and BADLY in (fish and presentation only - not safety); best tide state and size; best light (day / dusk / night); the species it is known for with their seasons; which of those are realistic in LATE NOVEMBER; proven methods and baits; size potential (specimens / records with dates); sources with dates and grades.
Remember human factors are irrelevant: include marks that are dangerous, inaccessible, private, closed or only reachable by boat or climbing (islands, stacks, cliff-foot ledges), and say what is known of the fishing there.
Outputs: (1) a readable markdown file with one section per mark, ordered geographically; (2) a JSONL file, one JSON object per mark, with exactly these keys: name, aliases (array), sector, nearest_town, lat, lon, coord_confidence (high|medium|low), type (surf beach|sheltered beach|steep shingle beach|rock mark|deep-water rock mark|headland|harbour|pier/breakwater/quay|estuary|tidal river|island/offshore rock|other), aspect_deg (compass degrees true that the mark faces, or null), ground, depth, features, fishes_well_in, fishes_badly_in, best_tide, best_light, species (array of {name, season, notes}), late_nov_species (array of names), methods_baits, size_potential, notes, sources (array of {url, title, pub_date, accessed, grade}), data_quality (good|moderate|thin).
Keep searching with new query angles (place names from the map, species + place, "mark", "ledge", "rock mark", "night fishing", club match reports) until two consecutive search rounds add no new marks. In your return summary give the number of marks, the best-evidenced late-November marks, and the stretches of your sector where you found NO documentation at all (name them precisely - these gaps matter).`

const INTEL_COMMON = `Collect RECENT, DATED shore-fishing intelligence for Cornwall and the Isles of Scilly. Priority 1: anything dated from about 10 October 2026 to today (21 Nov 2026) - catch reports, sightings of bait fish / birds working / tuna / dolphins close in, weed or water-colour reports, match results, tackle-shop reports. Priority 2: the same calendar window (late October to mid December) in 2023, 2024 and 2025 as seasonal analogues - clearly labelled as such. For each item record: the date of the catch/observation (and the publication date if different), venue as precisely as given, species, sizes/numbers, method/bait/lure, tide and conditions if stated, the source URL, the time you accessed it, and a source grade (B for clubs/shops/magazines, C for forum/social, etc.) plus your assessment of reliability (first-hand? photo? vague location to protect the mark?). Quote short key phrases verbatim. If a source cannot be reached (login walls, blocked) say so explicitly - do not guess its contents. Do not fabricate reports; an honest "nothing recent found for X" is a valid and useful result. In your return summary give the count of dated items by period, the strongest signals for what is being caught where right now, and the sources you could not access.`

