        key: 'sea_state', title: 'Sea state and water clarity (workstream 3)', verify: true, files: 'fishing/research/sea_state.md',
        prompt: `Deliver ${ROOT}/fishing/research/sea_state.md. Assess the sea ONLY for its effect on fish and fishing, never for angler safety. Scope:
1. Hourly wave forecast for 22 Nov 2026 (plus 21 and 23 Nov) at a nearshore sea point beside every STANDARD REFERENCE POINT: significant wave height, peak/mean period and direction; primary swell height/period/direction; wind-wave height/period/direction. Pull every wave model the Open-Meteo marine API exposes via "models=" (e.g. meteofrance_wave, meteofrance_currents for currents, ecmwf_wam025, ncep_gfswave025 / gfswave016, ewam, gwam, era5_ocean is not a forecast) and record which grid cell was returned (the API snaps to sea cells) and the model run. Tabulate model disagreement. Add other forecasts where reachable (Met Office, surf-forecast.com, Surfline, Windguru, PolarWRF etc.) for the main surf beaches.
2. Observations for the last 10-14 days: Channel Coastal Observatory wave buoys (Perranporth, Wave Hub, Penzance, Porthleven, Looe Bay, St Mary's Sound, Bideford Bay, Start Bay - coastalmonitoring.org real-time pages), Cefas WaveNet, Sevenstones lightship (NDBC 62107) and other Met Office moored buoys (62103 Channel, 62029 K1...) - Hs, Tp, direction, sea temperature. Build a day-by-day swell/storm history and identify the most recent big swell events and named storms.
3. Rainfall and river flow: EA flood-monitoring API river level stations on the Tamar (Gunnislake), Lynher, Tiddy, Looe rivers, Fowey (Restormel), Par/St Austell rivers, Fal, Tresillian, Kenwyn/Allen (Truro), Helford feeders, Cober (Helston), Hayle, Red River, Gannel, Menalhyl, Camel (Denby / Dunmere), Allen, Valency, Strat/Neet (Bude) - current level against typical range, and the trend over the last 10 days; rainfall totals. State what this means for freshwater colour and salinity in each estuary on 22 Nov.
4. Expected water clarity / colour on 22 Nov for EVERY coast sector and estuary (use the reference-point list as the sector list), with the reasoning: swell height and period breaking over sand vs rock, onshore vs offshore wind, run-off, tide size and resuspension, china-clay influence in St Austell Bay, plankton, plus any direct evidence - satellite ocean-colour/turbidity (try Copernicus / NASA Worldview / EUMETSAT true-colour for the most recent cloud-free pass), beach webcams, surf reports, divers' visibility reports, harbour notices. Grade each sector's clarity estimate (observed vs inferred) and flag THIN DATA.
5. Loose weed, surf "white water" extent, undertow / lateral current only as they affect bait presentation and fish location.
6. Translate into fishing terms per sector: e.g. "1.5 m, 12 s W swell on a W-facing surf beach = a fishable surf with coloured water, good for bass" versus "clear, calm = squid / wrasse / pollack conditions" - cite sources for each such rule of thumb (fishing literature, guides) and grade them.`,
        verifyHint: 'Re-pull wave forecasts for at least six points and two models; check the buoy observations against the source pages; re-pull at least eight river stations from the EA API and compare with the stated typical range; challenge every clarity claim that is presented as observed when it is really inferred.',
